          "content": "The problem asks for a single-evaluation evidence distinguishing genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance. All candidates so far propose within-evaluation perturbations or controls. But what if the distinguishing evidence is not a separate test, but a structural property of the error distribution itself? Specifically: if the panel's errors are geometrically orthogonal to the expert's errors in a shared feature space, that suggests the panel is extracting independent signal. But SRC_008 warns this is self-defeating. The real question is: what is the geometry of the error space? If the checklist is a linear projection, the panel's errors lie in the null space of that projection. The expert's errors lie in the full space. The question is whether the null space is predictive of the ground truth or not. If it is, the panel is missing signal; if it isn't, the panel has genuine gain. This is testable within a single evaluation by examining the residual of the expert's prediction after projecting onto the checklist's feature space.",
